      {
        "id": "read-model",
        "heading": "02 / Read the model, not a result",
        "accent": "violet",
        "text": [
          "The important dependency is genome supply → assembly → release. Translation separately makes proteins from RNA. The copying schematic is incoming +RNA → complementary RNA intermediate → new +RNA; each arrow compresses machinery and intermediate steps.",
          "At the shared gate, the numeric count is one completed new genome while the partial ribbon represents further synthesis in progress. The interruption stops that further synthesis. The cell wedge is a labeled pool symbol, not one mark per genome."
        ],
        "bullets": [
          "Incoming RNA: a pink pill labeled Incoming RNA. Completed new RNA: a cyan wedge labeled with its symbolic pool count. A spiky assembly mark represents its labeled pool; outside spiky marks are individual symbolic particles. The close-up uses a solid input ribbon and dashed emerging strand.",
          "This illustrative copying pathway takes place in the cytoplasm of a eukaryotic cell. Other organelles are omitted to focus the dependency; cell dimensions and membrane positions carry no anatomical scale.",
          "The alternate path assumes the completed pre-fork copy can be packaged and released with existing proteins. Real replication–assembly coupling varies. Three versus one outside particles are drawing choices, not measured yield, efficacy or a claim about every virus.",
          "Checkpoint: explain why protein production can continue without guaranteeing a supply of new complete genomes. Then identify which downstream stages the hypothetical interruption can constrain."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "evidence",
        "heading": "03 / Source and claim-limit ledger",
        "accent": "green",
        "text": [
          "These primary studies support selected mechanistic ideas. The generic enveloped lifecycle and alternate outcome are a teaching synthesis, not a reproduction of either study."
        ],
        "contract": {
          "title": "Evidence → permitted claim → boundary",
          "fields": [
            {
              "k": "Translation / replication",
              "v": "Gamarnik & Andino · Genes & Development 12, 2293–2304 (1998)",
              "g": "Supports distinguishing genome translation from RNA replication and a transition between those uses. Studied a specific non-enveloped virus; does not validate the envelope, entry route or universal timing in this composite.",
              "link": "https://genesdev.cshlp.org/content/12/15/2293.full"
            },
            {
              "k": "RNA synthesis",
              "v": "Wang et al. · Cell 182, 417–428.e13 (2020)",
              "g": "Structural work supports a polymerase engaging RNA during synthesis. This cartoon omits sequence, polarity detail, cofactors and measured kinetics; it does not depict the named virus studied in that paper.",
              "link": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0092867420306292"
            },
            {
              "k": "Accessible primary text",
              "v": "Wang et al. · full article archive",
              "g": "Accessible primary-source copy used to verify the polymerase claim. The hypothetical interruption here is not the intervention tested by that study.",
              "link": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7242921/"
            },
            {
              "k": "Authored hypothesis",
              "v": "Interrupt further synthesis → stop additional genome supply",
              "g": "One completed new RNA exists before the fork. The alternate assumes that copy can be packaged and released with existing proteins; the cited studies do not establish this generic output. Real replication–assembly coupling varies. Counts are neither yield nor efficacy estimates."
            },
            {
              "k": "Abstraction",
              "v": "One cell · symbolic genome supply · one branch point",
              "g": "The drawing omits complementary intermediates, compartment organization, competing processes, host responses and real timescales. Entry, packaging and release routes differ across viruses."
            }
          ]
        },
        "bullets": [
          "Rank 1 · Copy-gate comparison (chosen): keeps both paths identical until one defined dependency changes; strongest for separating translation from genome copying.",
          "Rank 2 · Entry-gate comparison: easier to show at the membrane, but removes the shared intracellular history and teaches a different dependency.",
          "Rank 3 · Assembly-gate comparison: makes residual RNA visible, but requires more structural detail and shifts attention away from RNA synthesis."
        ]
      }
